  "summary": "Fur-clad, bell-laden masked men chase winter demons through Balkan village streets.",
  "description": "Kukeri are masked male dancers who perform an ancient new-year rite across Thrace and the Balkan range: enormous wooden masks painted red and black, horns and beaks, layered ram-skin costumes heavy with strings of copper bells (chanove). Their leaping, ringing circuit drives away evil spirits and guarantees crops and fertility, a ritual remembered as Thracian Dionysian mumming and still exploded at Pernik's Surva festival, where hundreds of troupes from across the Balkans compete. Ritual contagion is the point — touching the kuker, tasting his wine, being chased by his bell-crash is the blessing.",
  "culturalContext": "Kukeri performance is Bulgaria's most living pre-Christian survival: the rite protects household and village across the Orthodox carnival calendar, survived both Ottoman rule and communist folklorization, and now anchors Pernik's Surva festival (UNESCO-listed) where troupes from the whole Balkan region compete. Wearing the mask is a village honor passed through families.",
